.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH DF 1M
.SH NAME
df \- disk free
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
[ filesystem ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Df
prints out the number of free blocks
available on the
.I filesystems.
If no file system is specified,
the free space on all of
the normally mounted file systems
is printed.
.PP
.B "Note:"
a
.I filesystem
here is the name of a
.IR device ,
not of a mount point: there is no
.IR /etc/mtab ,
.IR mount (1M)
not being ported.
The count is obtained by walking the free list itself, block by block, exactly as the
kernel's
.IR alloc ()
drains it.
The superblock's
.I s_tfree
field is
.B not
consulted and must not be: it is dead in this port and nothing maintains it.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 3072 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
a block on this machine is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- so every number
.I df
prints is one sixth of what the same filesystem would have shown on a PDP\-11.
There is no
.I BSHIFT
to go with it; 3072 is not a power of two.
.IR Ls (1)
counts the same block in its
.B \-s
and
.B total
output.
.SH "SUPER-USER ONLY"
.B "Note:"
.I df
reads the raw disk, and
.I /dev/rmd0
is mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
So this program works for the super-user and for nobody else.
It is deliberately
.B not
set-user-id: a set-uid
.I df
would hand the whole filesystem to anyone able to think of an offset.
.SH FILES
.I /dev/rmd0
\- the raw disk, and the default when no
.I filesystem
is named.
There is one EC\-5052 drive of 2000 blocks and there are no partitions; swap is on the
drums.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B "cannot open"
names a device that could not be read;
.I df
goes on to the next argument and exits 0 either way, which is v7's behaviour and is kept.
.PP
.B "bad free count"
and
.B "bad free block"
mean the free list is corrupt at the block named, and the number printed up to that point
is a lower bound rather than an answer.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
du(1), quot(1M), ls(1)
.br
.B "Note:"
v7's SEE ALSO named
.IR icheck (1M),
which is not ported.
